  "patterns": [
    {
      "id": "definite-noun-adjective",
      "pattern": "الـ + [Noun] + [Indefinite Adjective]",
      "exampleArabic": "الْبَيْتُ كَبِيرٌ",
      "exampleTransliteration": "al-baytu kabīrun",
      "exampleEnglish": "The house is big.",
      "grammarNote": "ال (al-) prefixes a noun to make it definite — 'the' — and it takes the noun's tanwīn ending away (بَيْتٌ 'a house' → الْبَيْتُ 'the house'). A definite subject followed by an indefinite adjective is still a complete equational sentence, same pattern as level 5's هَذَا بَيْتٌ. Two orthography quirks worth knowing: (1) Arabic letters split into 'sun letters' (ت ث د ذ ر ز س ش ص ض ط ظ ل ن) and 'moon letters' (everything else). Before a sun letter, the ل of ال is silent and the sun letter doubles instead — الشَّمْسُ is pronounced ash-shamsu, not al-shamsu. Before a moon letter (like ق in القمر), the ل is pronounced normally: al-qamaru. (2) تاء مربوطة (ة), seen in الْمَرْأَةُ, is silent — pronounced as a light '-ah' at the end of a phrase.",
      "audio": { "id": "phrase_albaytu_kabiirun", "text": "الْبَيْتُ كَبِيرٌ" },
      "introducedInLevel": 11
    }
  ],
